Counters that separate the digest work of code signatures with 16 KiB hash pages from the rest of its window.

The "hash" time of these signatures (cs_native4k_hash_ticks) is wall time summed over every thread inside the window at
once. In the lazy form the window also holds the allocation and protection of a chunk of answers and the lock that
records an answer, and in both forms any time the thread waited for a processor. It therefore says nothing about the
speed of SHA-256 itself. The digests are those of the code directory's hash type through libkern (SHA256_Init and its
kin), which call g_crypto_funcs: the corecrypto of the boot kernel collection, registered at STARTUP SYSCTL before any
signature is added, whose SHA-256 is its ARMv8 SHA2 implementation.

This adds, per original 16 KiB block, around the digests of the block and of its 4 KiB parts only: the wall ticks, the
ticks the thread itself ran (recount_current_thread_time_mach) and the bytes hashed, by the type of the core the block
ended on (efficiency cores, the others); and in the lazy form the chunks made, the ticks spent making them and the ticks
spent recording answers. sysctl vm.cs_native4k_stats gains "sha E <MiB>/<wall ms>/<cpu ms> P ... chunks <n> <ms>
record <ms>". Nothing that is read, hashed, accepted or refused changes.

Has not run on hardware.
